window.docContents[192].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-the-unlabelled-count":{"contents":"Taking n = k, the cube Q[k] has 2 ^ k * k! automorphisms (as we already knew\nfrom card_aut_hypercube), and dividing the labelled count by this number gives the\nunlabelled count.\n\nThe number of subgraphs of Q[n] isomorphic to Q[k]: choose the k free\ncoordinates, then fix the remaining n - k coordinates arbitrarily./-- The number of subgraphs of Q[n] isomorphic to Q[k]: choose the k free\ncoordinates, then fix the remaining n - k coordinates arbitrarily. -/\ntheorem copyCount_hypercube (n k : ℕ) (h : k ≤ n) :\n    Q[n].copyCount Q[k] = n.choose k * 2 ^ (n - k) := by\n  have hcopy : Q[k].labelledCopyCount Q[k] = 2 ^ k * k.factorial := by\n    rw [labelledCopyCount_hypercube, Nat.descFactorial_self]\n  have hlabel := labelledCopyCount_hypercube n k\n  rw [labelledCopyCount_eq_copyCount_mul_labelledCopyCount,\n    Nat.descFactorial_eq_factorial_mul_choose, hcopy] at hlabel\n  have h2 : 2 ^ n = 2 ^ k * 2 ^ (n - k) := by\n    rw [← pow_add, Nat.add_sub_cancel' h]\n  rw [h2] at hlabel\n  -- positivity proves goals 0 < e or 0 ≤ e from the form of e\n  refine Nat.eq_of_mul_eq_mul_right (by positivity : 0 < 2 ^ k * k.factorial) ?_\n  rw [hlabel]\n  ring\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory\u0009Subcubes of the Hypercube*","header":"12.8.5. The Unlabelled Count","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-the-unlabelled-count"},"/Connectivity/#section-maximum-degree-two":{"contents":"The case Δ = 2 combines two earlier results. A graph is 2-colourable iff it has\nno odd cycle (isBipartite_iff_forall_isCycle_even), and in a graph of maximum\ndegree at most 2 every cycle is a whole connected component\n(Walk.IsCycle.toSubgraph_eq_of_degree_le_two). So a graph of maximum\ndegree at most 2 is 2-colourable unless one of its connected components is an odd\ncycle. Indeed, if it is not 2-colourable then it has an odd cycle c, based at u\nsay, and by\nWalk.IsCycle.toSubgraph_eq_of_degree_le_two that cycle is its component:\nc.toSubgraph = G⟦u⟧.toSubgraph. This equation is how we state that a component is\nan odd cycle.\n\nBrooks' theorem (Δ = 2): a graph with all degrees at most 2 is 2-colourable\nunless it has a component that is an odd cycle./-- *Brooks' theorem (Δ = 2)*: a graph with all degrees at most 2 is 2-colourable\nunless it has a component that is an odd cycle. -/\ntheorem brooks_two [G.LocallyFinite] (hdeg : ∀ v, G.degree v ≤ 2)\n    (hcyc : ¬ ∃ (u : V) (c : G.Walk u u),\n      c.IsCycle ∧ Odd c.length ∧ c.toSubgraph = G⟦u⟧.toSubgraph) :\n    G.Colorable 2 := by\n  apply isBipartite_iff_forall_isCycle_even.mpr\n  intro u c hc\n  by_contra hodd\n  exact hcyc ⟨u, c, hc, Nat.not_even_iff_odd.mp hodd,\n    hc.toSubgraph_eq_of_degree_le_two hdeg⟩\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Connectivity\u0009Brooks' Theorem*","header":"10.5.1. Maximum Degree Two","id":"/Connectivity/#section-maximum-degree-two"}});